From 54748e0d93fd183f809b597cacb00b6b216f0aac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 17:47:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][DVBridge] Every frame sent as the first picture of a
 scene, a comparison

A user with an Intel GPU, a Denon receiver and a Sony A80K saw native
Dolby Vision look brighter with another build. What leaves the PC was
compared byte by byte: the InfoFrames are the same (the AVI of the
tunnel RGB, full range, no colorimetry; the Dolby VSIF with the signal
bit only; the SPD of a digital set-top box), and so is the metadata of a
real profile 7 FEL RPU but for one byte: scene_refresh_flag, which that
build sets in every frame. Hades sends the flag of the RPU, as a
licensed player does (a capture of one sends it only at the cuts).
ETSI GS CCM 001 6.2.2 has the flag say that a picture is the first of a
scene: the display starts its mapping again from that frame, without
its filtering between the frames of a scene. Whether a display looks
different with it is only seen on the display.

So that it can be seen, advancedsettings.xml can ask for every frame to
go out with the flag set (<video><dvscenerefreshall>true
</dvscenerefreshall></video>; dvbridge_set_refresh_all()). The rest of
each payload is the same; a scene whose metadata do not change keeps its
metadata id; a new stream keeps the switch, as it keeps the trims
setting; kodi.log says when it is on. Off by default: the default stays
what a licensed player sends.
